{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":243327,"duration_api_ms":301473,"num_turns":26,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0036c44a-5f15-4861-848e-3b6127013866","total_cost_usd":1.1362755000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":3940,"cache_creation_input_tokens":56229,"cache_read_input_tokens":711411,"output_tokens":4884,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":56229,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":3940,"outputTokens":4884,"cacheReadInputTokens":711411,"cacheCreationInputTokens":56229,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.84893675,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":2557,"outputTokens":15455,"cacheReadInputTokens":854605,"cacheCreationInputTokens":97637,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.28733875000000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In `egg_dropping()` (shrimp.rs:800-806), egg-drop resolution iterates per-female with `state.rng.next_f64()` inside a `for _ in 0..cohort.count` loop. For a large colony (hundreds of berried females across many cohorts), this is O(N) RNG calls per day. Replace with a binomial draw or deterministic fractional transfer (as used for spawning and hatching) to avoid the per-female loop and keep performance consistent with the rest of the reproduction pipeline.","rationale":"The spawning and hatching paths already use `deterministic_transfer_count` which is O(1) per cohort. Egg dropping is the only reproduction sub-step that uses per-individual RNG rolls, creating an inconsistency in the simulation model (deterministic elsewhere, stochastic here) and a potential performance hotspot at scale. For a simulation that runs thousands of hours, this matters."},{"body":"The egg drop probability cap of 0.6 (shrimp.rs:793) is hardcoded. Extract to a named species parameter (e.g., `egg_drop_max_probability`) in `neocaridina_davidi.toml` and `ShrimpRuntimeParams`, consistent with the acceptance criterion that all suppression factors be named parameters.","rationale":"AC #8 (`test_all_reproduction_factors_are_named_parameters`) requires that all reproduction tuning knobs be in species/process data. The 0.6 cap directly controls egg-dropping severity but is not tunable without a code change. The test does not verify this specific value is parameterized."},{"body":"The dissolved oxygen reference value of 6.0 mg/L in `egg_development()` (shrimp.rs:876) is hardcoded: `(do_mg_l / 6.0).clamp(0.0, 1.0)`. Extract to a named species parameter (e.g., `egg_oxygen_reference_mg_l`) in `ShrimpRuntimeParams` and `neocaridina_davidi.toml`.","rationale":"This value directly controls how dissolved oxygen suppresses hatch success. Unlike TAN/NO2/density/temperature which all use parameterized thresholds from the TOML, the oxygen factor uses a magic number. This violates the spirit of AC #8 and makes DO sensitivity untunable without code changes."},{"body":"The reproductive readiness EMA smoothing rate of 0.1 (shrimp.rs:694) is hardcoded. Extract to a named species parameter (e.g., `reproductive_readiness_smoothing`) in `ShrimpRuntimeParams`. This constant controls how quickly shrimp respond to environmental changes in their breeding readiness — a key tuning lever for the 'lingering suppression after destabilization' behavior.","rationale":"The instability tracker already has parameterized rise/decay smoothing rates (`instability_rise_smoothing`, `instability_decay_smoothing`). The reproductive readiness EMA serves the same kind of role but its rate is hardcoded, creating an asymmetry. The AC requires instability to suppress reproduction 'for 2-3 days after the disturbance' — tuning this requires changing the smoothing rate, which currently requires a code change."},{"body":"The `clutch_condition_modifier()` function (shrimp.rs:1000) uses a hardcoded threshold of 0.7 for full clutch size. Extract to a named parameter (e.g., `full_clutch_condition_threshold`) alongside the existing `min_clutch_condition` parameter to make the clutch-size curve fully tunable.","rationale":"The linear ramp from `min_clutch_condition` (0.3, parameterized) to 0.7 (hardcoded) defines how condition affects clutch size. Only half of this curve is tunable. Since the AC requires condition to be a named, inspectable factor, the full curve shape should be parameterized."},{"body":"The raw instability computation in `update_stability_tracker()` (shrimp.rs:203-207) sums four normalized swing components and clamps to [0, 1]. However, with four additive terms each potentially reaching 1.0, the theoretical max before clamping is 4.0. This means any single moderate swing (e.g., temp swing = instability_temp_swing_c) already drives raw_instability to 1.0 if any other chemistry moved at all. Consider either (a) dividing by the number of components (4) to normalize, or (b) using a weighted max instead of sum, so individual swings have predictable contribution to the overall instability index.","rationale":"The additive formulation means a temperature swing of `instability_temp_swing_c` (3°C) alone contributes 1.0 and immediately saturates the instability index. Multiple small swings across different parameters also saturate easily. This makes the decay/rise smoothing parameters less meaningful because raw_instability frequently hits the 1.0 clamp. The test for instability suppression (AC #2) works only because the EMA smoothing attenuates the spike, but the underlying signal has poor dynamic range."}],"summary":"Reviewed the reproduction success model implementation across shrimp.rs (factor functions, spawning, egg dropping, egg development, clutch sizing), process/species parameters in TOML files, and all 14 tests in reproduction_factors.rs plus supporting test files. All tests pass. The core multiplicative factor model is sound: 8 factors for readiness, 7 for hatch rate, with proper division-by-zero guards, deterministic spawning/hatching via carry accumulators, and good diagnostic output. All 10 acceptance criteria tests are present and meaningful. Key issues found: (1) egg_dropping uses per-female RNG rolls instead of the deterministic transfer pattern used everywhere else — inconsistent and O(N); (2) several magic numbers that should be named parameters per AC #8 (DO reference 6.0, egg drop cap 0.6, readiness EMA 0.1, clutch condition floor 0.7); (3) raw instability computation sums 4 normalized components but clamps to [0,1], so dynamic range is poor — single moderate swings saturate the index. No correctness bugs or safety issues found. The thermal_reproduction_penalty.rs test has a loose `<=` assertion but it's in a separate older test file and doesn't affect the AC coverage in reproduction_factors.rs."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"3823bbf6-7d58-4eaa-bc8a-b3dfd07e8111"}
